THE THING THIS RUN ACTUALLY ESTABLISHED
---------------------------------------
Three measurements, in order, each refuting the previous plan:

1. `probe_borrow_cost` (microbenchmark, median of 9) prices ONE acquire+release
   pair at 7.89 ns, of which 1.25 ns is the guard with no tracker.  The shipped
   three-RMW sequence measured 3.50 ns in isolation against 1.54 ns for one RMW
   plus plain stores.  15.65 M borrow pairs per frame (`probe-count`) x 2.0 ns
   predicted about 26 ms/frame from deleting two of the three RMWs.

2. Deleting them measured **-0.6% at 8bpc t=1**, not -7%.  A tight dependent
   loop exposes an RMW's latency; the decoder's out-of-order window hides it.
   AN ISOLATED INSTRUCTION COST DOES NOT TRANSFER TO AN OoO PIPELINE WHERE THAT
   INSTRUCTION IS OFF THE CRITICAL PATH.

3. Removing 26 more instructions (the whole stack frame) measured null on the
   same cell.  So the plan changed again, and `probe-addnop` was built to
   settle it: keep the call, delete the body.  It landed at 290.97 against
   365.77 for the real tracker and 287.11 for no tracker — i.e. **the call
   barrier is 4.4% of the tracker's cost and 95.6% is `add`'s own latency**,
   even though neither the RMW count nor the instruction count was buying
   anything.

   What was left is the DEPENDENCY CHAIN at the head of `add`: a load of
   `self.shift`, two shifts, a 64-bit multiply and two masks, all feeding the
   address the lock acquire needs.  On a `mask == 0` instance every one of
   those is dead — `shard_of(b, 0)` is 0 for every block.  Skipping them is
   the `final` arm and it is worth **-4.5% (8bpc t=1) / -5.4% (10bpc t=1)**,
   bands disjoint, roughly seven times what the RMW and frame work bought
   together.

Everything that shipped after that point is the same lever applied again,
and every one of them paid: `live_mask` and `alloc` straight-lined for the
measured steady state (occ_max == 1, mean 0.02), `allocated` stored once
instead of twice, `mutable` stored whole instead of read-modify-written.
Tracker cost at 8bpc t=1 (base - addnop): 72.0 -> 46.9 ms/frame, **-34.9%**.

Generalisation worth keeping: on this path, LATENCY fixes pay and THROUGHPUT
fixes do not.  Anything that shortens the chain from `self` to the lock
acquire, or from the lock acquire to the record store, is worth measuring;
anything that merely removes instructions from around it is not.


MEASURED NEGATIVES (do not redo without a contradicting profile)
----------------------------------------------------------------
fused lock+occupancy word (fetch_or to acquire, fetch_xor to publish+release)
    4.76 ns/pair vs 3.50 for the shipped triple.  Three RMWs on ONE address
    serialise; the shipped ones are on two addresses and overlap.  MICRO ONLY.
publish with Relaxed instead of Release (the lock already orders it)
    4.82 ns/pair vs 3.50.  Slower, repeatably.  MICRO ONLY.
`find` early-out on `occupied == 0`, to skip the `mutable` load (`findeo`)
    +2.3% at 8bpc t=1 (335.1 -> 342.7, median of 9, idle).  The load is free
    (same cache line, issued in parallel) and LLVM already folds the test into
    one `ands`+`b.eq`; the early-out adds a second branch with the same
    outcome.  DECODE-LEVEL.
dropping the pre-lock `state` check now that the in-lock re-read routes to the
same cold path (`chain3`, measured composed with findeo)
    +0.8% at 8bpc t=1 over `chain2`.  That load warms the header line and
    resolves its branch while the lock acquire is in flight; removing it
    serialises the in-lock load behind the acquire instead of shortening
    anything.  DECODE-LEVEL.
duplicating the body to constant-fold shard 0's address (`fold`)
    +0.9% (8bpc t=1) / +0.8% (10bpc t=1) vs `final`, disjoint bands, idle box,
    n=9.  The address does fold; the function goes 117 -> 208 instructions and
    gets its stack frame back, and that costs more.  DECODE-LEVEL.
two of three locked RMWs, alone (`slotflag`)
    -1.1% (8bpc t=1) / -2.1% (10bpc t=1).  Kept — every one of 8 cells is
    below 1.000 and the mechanism removes two `lock`-prefixed RMWs per pair,
    which on x86-64 are full fences where Apple LSE atomics are near-free
    (#458).  NOT MEASURED ON x86-64 — do not assume the M4 number transfers.
frameless leaf, alone (`leaf` minus `slotflag`)
    -0.5% (8bpc t=1) / -0.7% (10bpc t=1).  Kept for the same reason; it is
    also what makes the cold paths cold.


CANDIDATE 4 (stop reserving the strided hull in `block_mut`/`narrow_guard`,
split into row-groups) IS REFUTED, not untested
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
`probe-wide` on the shipped build, v4k_8tile 8bpc, per frame:

    t=1   slow 0        multi 0        wide_total 0
    t=8   slow 17,275   multi 17,275   wide_total 0

At t=1 the strided hull costs EXACTLY what a one-byte borrow costs: `mask == 0`
so the fast path takes it whole, without the block walk, without a multi-shard
registration, and `find`'s cost is O(live slots) and independent of extent.
Splitting a hull into row-groups there can only ADD registrations (h x more)
for zero saving.  At t=8 the whole multi-block population is 17,275 of about
15.65 M adds per frame — 0.11% — so eliminating it entirely cannot be worth
more than a tenth of a percent.  The original motivation (feeding the wide
path) died with #458; this measures that the replacement motivation
(extent-driven scan cost) never existed.


CANDIDATE 3 (thread-local registries) — COSTED, NOT BUILT
----------------------------------------------------------
Priced in `probe_borrow_cost`: a publish + `dmb ish` + one peer scan + release
is **0.599 ns/pair** against 3.50 ns for the shipped atomic sequence, and the
`dmb ish` alone is **0.246 ns** — a full fence is nearly free on this core,
which is what makes the shape viable at all.  So the ceiling is real.

Two things make it a redesign rather than an increment, and both are load-
bearing:

* The scan is O(registrants), over lines those registrants WRITE on every
  borrow.  Measured: all 8 workers touch all 12 hot plane instances, so at t=8
  this is 7 coherence misses per borrow.  It is only cheap where it is
  currently needed most (t=1) and catastrophic where the scaling deficit is
  (t=8).  A registrant-count fast path ("if only I have ever registered on this
  instance, scan nothing") is what makes it usable — and that is the hard part.
* That fast path is a store-buffer (Dekker) problem: a joining thread and an
  incumbent can each publish then scan and both miss.  Fixing it needs SeqCst
  on the fast path (which is the cost we were trying to avoid) or an RCU-style
  grace period on the join, where the joiner waits until every incumbent has
  passed a quiescence point.  The latter keeps the fast path at 0.599 ns and
  puts all the cost on a once-per-thread-per-instance join.

Estimate: ~46.9 ms/frame is what the tracker still costs at 8bpc t=1, and this
shape could plausibly take most of it — 337 ms would become ~300, i.e. ~1.22x,
which is the only identified route past the 1.30x bar at t=1.  It is a
multi-day change to the crate's core invariant and it must not be attempted
without the mutation battery below extended to cover the join protocol.


SOUNDNESS
---------
Four hazards planted, each confirmed to FAIL the suite, each restored with a
0-line `git diff`, green after every restore:
  (a) delete `add`'s in-lock `state` re-read (the 4af62ae wide-path TOCTOU)
      -> wide_exclusion FAILS on `witnesses`, 1206 missed overlaps
  (b) `active()` cut to one shard
      -> wide_exclusion FAILS on `witnesses`, 3 missed overlaps
  (c) a published slot never enters `recs.allocated` (NEW invariant)
      -> FAILS (6 tests on the first shape of the change, 3 on the shipped
         one), incl. same_block_overlap_is_caught and
         cross_shard_overlaps_are_all_caught
  (d) `recs.allocated` narrowed to 0, dropping LIVE slots (NEW invariant)
      -> FAILS, shard_overflow_promotes_and_still_detects.  The publish
         immediately re-sets the bit, so the invariant can only be broken for a
         slot that stays live ACROSS another registration — which is exactly
         the shape that test builds, and the reason it is the only one that
         catches this.
All four run under `--features __probe_wide`, which is what makes the
wide_exclusion gate non-vacuous (f968094, 6c17d8c).

